"""Content B: Gods & Ruling Powers, Völva (Volva) & Seiðr (Seidr), Hamingja, Vitki."""

CH3 = [
('lore', 'Rǫgn: Gods', 'Æsir and Vanir: The God Tribes', ["Rǫgn is an old Norse word for the powers: the gods and the forces that rule the world. The gods come in two families. The Æsir include Óðinn, Þórr, and Frigg. They live in Ásgarðr, the high realm in the sky, and they care about oath, battle, and order. The Vanir include Njörðr, Freyr, and Freyja. They live in Vanaheimr and care about the sea, the harvest, and plenty. Giants and elves live in other realms and belong to neither family.",
  "The two families once fought a war and then made peace. The next page tells how it ended. Scholars think the story may remember two peoples who later joined together. It comes to us from the Völuspá, the seeress’s prophecy in the Poetic Edda, and from Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, written around 1220. Both were written down centuries after the Viking Age, so the details are uncertain.",
  "Think of the two families as two households that learned to share one valley. Each keeps its own job. The Æsir keep oath and order, and the Vanir keep work and plenty. A home that has only one of them is missing something.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Æsir and Vanir are in the Völuspá (Poetic Edda) and Snorri’s Prose Edda, both copied late. The idea that they were once two peoples is a scholars’ theory, not proven."], "Which job is missing in your home right now: oath and order, or work and plenty? Write one dated line about it and what you will do this week."),
('lore', 'Gods', "The Æsir–Vanir war", ["The Æsir and Vanir once fought a war. The Völuspá says the Æsir broke a truce, and the Vanir fought back. Neither side could win for good, so they made peace.",
  "The peace was built on hostages, a person from each side sent to live with the other. Njörðr and Freyr went to live among the Æsir. Hænir (Hoenir) and Mímir (Mimir) went to the Vanir. A hostage was a guest, fed and treated well, and that gave both sides a reason to keep their word. A promise is easy to break. A person sitting at the other table is harder to forget.",
  "Njörðr and Freyr spent time in Ásgarðr as part of the treaty, but their home is Vanaheimr. The stories do not describe life after the treaty, so think of it this way: each family has its own home and its own job, and they meet at the table. The Völuspá and Snorri’s Prose Edda tell this as medieval Icelandic story, not as history of a real war.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The war and the hostage exchange are told in the Völuspá and Snorri’s Prose Edda, both copied late."], "Name one quarrel in your life and the person on the other side. Invite them to one shared meal this month, and write one dated line about it."),
('lore', 'Gods of Ásgarðr', 'Óðinn: the one-eyed', ["Óðinn (Odin) is the chief of the Æsir, the high one, and the god of wisdom, poetry, war, and the dead. He rides the eight-legged horse Sleipnir, shown on picture stones and thin gold medallions called bracteates. Two ravens, Huginn and Muninn, fly out each day and bring him news: thought and memory. Two wolves, Geri and Freki, follow at his heels. The Eddas also call him Alföðr (Allfather).",
  "He paid a high price for wisdom. He gave one eye to the well of Mímir (Mimir) for a drink of its water. He hung nine nights on Yggdrasill, wounded by his own spear, to win the runes. The Hávamál gives his long counsel on wits and measure. The Poetic Edda shows him generous and terrible by turns, and he breaks oaths too.",
  "Later books make him a kindly Allfather for modern worship, which is a modern softening. Jackson Crawford, a modern scholar of Old Norse, has argued that the name Alföðr may mean ‘All Order’ rather than ‘All-Father’. This reading is debated, and this book does not settle it.",
  "The lesson is in the price. Óðinn wants to know things, and that hunger never comes free. Steady attention counts for more than an altar nobody visits. Give a set hour each week to learning a real craft, a poem, or a history.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Óðinn’s eye, hanging, ravens, wolves, and horse are in the Poetic Edda, Snorri’s Prose Edda, and on stones. The ‘All Order’ reading is Jackson Crawford’s, and it is debated."], 'What hard thing will you learn for Óðinn? Name the poem or craft or history, the hour, and twice this week. Give it two dated lines.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Ásgarðr', 'Óðinn — practice', ["The practice is study, not asking. A chapter of the Hávamál in translation can serve. So can learning a row of runes one letter at a time, or reading Egils saga (Egil’s Saga) ten pages at a sitting. Sit at the same hour twice a week and stay long enough for the work to sink in. Learn names and verses first. Patience is the manners of this practice.",
  "The offering is attention, not a deal. Nothing is bargained for. Copying verses by hand fits here, and so does memorizing three stanzas. These are reconstructed gestures, built on the old pattern of seeking counsel. Jackson Crawford puts it this way: “As Odin sought wisdom from the dead, so too do we seek wisdom from those who have passed when we read their words.”",
  "Keep each session under half an hour, with your hands free and your phone away. At the end, write one line on what you learned. Coming back is the vow you keep.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The study practice was rebuilt for this book from the old pattern. The quotation is Jackson Crawford’s."], 'Name the study you will keep twice this week and the hour you will keep it. Give it under half an hour each time with phone away. Give it two dated lines on what you learned.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Ásgarðr', 'Frigg: wife and seer', ["Frigg is the wife of Óðinn and the mother of Baldr. She sits beside the high seat and sees further than she tells. She knows what will come and speaks of it to almost no one. She spins and weaves and keeps the keys and inner doors of the household.",
  "Two stories show her. In the Lokasenna, a poem in the Poetic Edda, she warns Loki that Baldr would have made him pay for his insults, and Loki answers that he is the reason Baldr never rides home. In Snorri’s Prose Edda, when Baldr dreams of his death, she makes everything in the world swear not to harm him. She works by foresight and preparation.",
  "Some scholars think Frigg and Freyja may be two names for one goddess. Here Frigg holds marriage and household foresight, and Freyja holds love and growth. In your home, Frigg stands for planning ahead. Look at what winter will ask of your home: lamp oil, savings, rest. Lay one thing ready this week, and cool quarrels before they catch fire.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Frigg’s foresight is in the Poetic Edda, and her oath-taking for Baldr is in Snorri’s Prose Edda. Scholars debate whether she and Freyja were once one goddess."], 'Look ahead one season: what does your household see coming, and what will you lay ready? Name lamp oil or savings or rest and lay one thing ready this week. Give it two dated lines.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Vanaheimr', 'Freyja of Fólkvangr', ["Freyja is the lady of the Vanir, and her name means “lady.” She takes half of those slain in battle to her own field, Fólkvangr, and the other half goes to Óðinn. She owns the necklace Brísingamen (Brisingamen), drives a cart drawn by cats, and wears a feather cloak that lets her fly. She knows the craft of love and of fate.",
  "In the Þrymskviða (Thrymskvida), the giant Þrymr steals Þórr’s hammer and demands Freyja as his bride. She refuses to be traded, and her anger shakes the halls. Þórr dresses as the bride instead and gets the hammer back. Some scholars think Freyja and Frigg may once have been one goddess, but this book keeps their roles separate.",
  "She stands for plenty and for the right to choose. You may refuse a bad deal outright, and welcome a fair one that brings growth. Say no to any deal that treats you as a price, and keep your worth steady through hard weeks.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Fólkvangr, the necklace, and the feather cloak are in the Poetic Edda. The cat-cart is in Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda. Scholars debate whether Freyja and Frigg were once one goddess."], 'Name a time you refused to be traded. Name what you kept and how you kept it. Give it three dated lines with the refusal plain.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Vanaheimr', 'Freyr of harvest and peace', ["Freyr is the Vanir lord of harvest, fair weather, and peace, and his name means “lord.” He owns the ship Skíðblaðnir (Skidbladnir), which folds up like cloth, and the boar Gullinbursti, with golden bristles. Kings called him an ancestor, and farmers called on him for rain.",
  "In the Skírnismál (Skirnismal), Freyr falls in love with Gerðr (Gerd), a giant maid, and sends his servant Skírnir to win her with gifts and then threats. To get her, Freyr gives away his own sword, and Snorri says he goes to Ragnarök without it. Adam of Bremen later describes a fertility god at Uppsala, usually read as Freyr. That account is late and written from a Christian point of view.",
  "His gifts come with a limit. Plenty can turn into greed. Ask yourself what you grew that deserves sharing this season. Pick one loaf, one jar, or one mended thing. Show it to one witness and share it the same day.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Skírnismál is in the Poetic Edda. The Uppsala account is late and written from a Christian point of view."], 'What increase deserves sharing this season? Name the loaf or jar or mended thing and the witness. Share it the same day with two dated lines.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Ásgarðr', 'Þórr: driver of the goat cart', ["Þórr (Thor) is the thunder god of the Æsir and the strongest of them. He drives a cart pulled by two goats, Tanngnjóstr (Tooth-Gnasher) and Tanngrisnir (Tooth-Gritter), and the cart throws sparks as it goes. He has a red beard and the hammer Mjölnir (Mjollnir). He guards Miðgarðr, the world of people, against the giants.",
  "In the Þrymskviða poem a giant steals his hammer, and Þórr dresses as a bride to win it back. The hammer is used to bless the bride, and small hammer amulets show that people in the north wore it for protection. His job, as the sources show it, is guarding the edges of life: a wedding, the border of a field, and the launch of a ship. The practice below is a reconstruction built on that pattern.",
  "In your own life, this is the work of keeping the boundaries other people rely on. Think of his cart stopping at your gate. The gate latch gets fixed, the inbox gets sorted, and the bedtime routine stays steady. Walk one errand in bad weather. Mending a thing keeps faith with the people who depend on it.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Þórr’s hammer, goats, and hallowing are in the Poetic Edda and Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, and hammer amulets survive."], "Who does the sheltering in your life? Name the boundary you most depend on and the mending that is yours this week. Give it gate or inbox or bedtime with a date. Give it two dated lines."),
('lore', "Gods", "Skaði: winter's bride", ["Skaði (Skadi) is a giantess of the mountains and winter, a hunter on skis with a bow. When the gods kill her father, the giant Þjazi (Thiazi), she arms herself and strides into Ásgarðr to take revenge.",
  "The gods offer her a settlement instead. She may choose a husband from among them, but she can see only their feet. She picks the handsomest feet, hoping for Baldr, and finds she has chosen Njörðr, the sea god. The gods also make her laugh, when Loki ties a cord between himself and a goat. Óðinn throws Þjazi’s eyes into the sky as stars. The marriage is short. She loves the mountains and he loves the sea, so they part, each to their own shore.",
  "Þórr himself boasts of killing Þjazi in the Harbarðsljóð (Harbardsljod). Read her as a story about terms kept. The price is named and paid, and each person lives where they thrive. Plain terms settle a bargain: say what is owed, say what will settle it, and say which place each party keeps afterward.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Skaði’s settlement is in Snorri’s Prose Edda, and Þórr’s boast is in the Harbarðsljóð, both late. The household use was written for this book."], "What bargain of yours needs plain terms, and which shore will each party keep? Name what is owed and what settles it. Give it three dated lines and speak it before the week ends."),
('lore', 'Gods', 'Loki: The Trickster', ["No devil stands here, whatever later sermons made of him. Snorri’s Gylfaginning says Loki was born a giant, the son of Fárbauti and Laufey, and the old texts sometimes name him for his mother, Laufey’s son. He lives among the Æsir in Ásgarðr, and in the Lokasenna he reminds Óðinn that the two swore blood-brotherhood. With the giantess Angrboða he fathered Hel, the wolf Fenrir, and the world serpent. His mischief starts small and turns costly. In Snorri’s Prose Edda he cuts off the golden hair of Sif, the wife of Þórr, and Þórr threatens to break every bone in him. Loki goes to the dwarfs for new hair, and the gifts he brings back include Þórr’s hammer. The harm grows. He arranges the death of Baldr, and in the Lokasenna he insults every god at a feast until Þórr threatens him with his hammer and he leaves. At last the gods bind him in a cave with a serpent’s venom dripping on him. At Ragnarök he breaks free. He is clever, cruel, and useful.", 'He is cleverness without loyalty, and he cannot be flattered into favors. If trouble with him comes near, bring repair and nothing else. Own the mess and repair it fully, facing the person you hurt directly. The binding and the insults are told in the Poetic Edda and Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda. Soft modern readings that make him a misunderstood friend belong elsewhere, not here.', 'Clear thinking does cold work here, like a knife paring rot from wood. List your tangles. Strike the ones that are only noise. Keep the one that is truly a mess to mend. Clarity is the only answer he respects. One cleanly repaired tangle is worth more than a year of excuses.', '<b>Where this comes from:</b> Loki’s parentage and the story of Sif are in Snorri’s Prose Edda. His blood-brotherhood with Óðinn and his binding are in the Lokasenna.'], 'Sort it out: which tangle is your mess to mend, and which is only noise? Name the one mess. Give it one dated line tonight.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Ásgarðr', 'Baldr: The Light', ["Baldr is the shining son of Óðinn and Frigg, and the best loved of the gods. The sources tell little about him, so this page is short on purpose. His dreams show him doomed. His mother makes everything in the world swear not to harm him, but she overlooks the mistletoe. Loki guides the hand of Baldr’s blind brother Höðr (Hodr), who shoots him with it.",
  "All things mourn Baldr, but he is held in Hel after the ransom weeping fails. Hermóðr (Hermod) rides down to ask for him back and returns empty-handed. Grief is most of what the sources give him.",
  "He gets one page and one use: the beloved who is lost. Claims that he is a dying god like others around the world, or a Christ-like figure, are modern, and this book sets them aside without scorn. We all lose something that brought us light, and Baldr can stand for that. Keep one grief on the page all month, without forcing it to teach you anything. Name the missed person in one true sentence.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Baldr’s death is in the Poetic Edda and Snorri’s Prose Edda, copied late. The idea of a dying god is a modern comparison."], 'What beloved loss sits with you still? Name who is missed in one true sentence. Give it a day and one dated line.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Ásgarðr', 'Týr (Tyr) of the oath', ["Týr (Tyr) is the god of oaths and law, and a god of battle and victory. He is best known for losing his right hand. The gods wanted to bind the wolf Fenrir, Loki’s son, who had grown too strong. Fenrir would let them tie him only if one of them put a hand in his mouth as a pledge. Týr did, and when the chain held and Fenrir could not break free, the wolf bit the hand off.",
  "The story is told in Snorri’s Prose Edda, and the lost hand is named in the Lokasenna. The Sigrdrífumál and the medieval rune poems (Norwegian, Icelandic, and Anglo-Saxon) name his rune for victory. Þórr guards by strength, and Týr guards by oath.",
  "Choose one pledge this week. Make it short enough to keep without footnotes. Say it to one witness, state the cost, and keep it exactly. A kept promise is what makes your word trusted tomorrow.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The binding of Fenrir is in Snorri’s Prose Edda, and Týr’s rune is in the rune poems and the Sigrdrífumál."], 'Which oath of yours needs a hand behind it? Name it short enough to keep without footnotes. Speak it to one witness and give it one dated line.'),
('lore', 'Gods of Ásgarðr', 'Heimdallr: watchman', ["Heimdallr (Heimdall) is the watchman of the gods. He guards Bifröst, the rainbow bridge to Ásgarðr, and he hardly sleeps. His hearing is so keen he can hear grass grow. He owns the horn Gjallarhorn, which will sound at the last day. He was born of nine mothers at the edge of the sea, as the Hyndluljóð (Hyndluljod) tells it.",
  "In the Lokasenna he tells Loki he is drunk and should go home. Snorri says the two will kill each other at Ragnarök, watcher against trickster.",
  "His lesson is the watch. Keep one bridge faithfully: a door, an inbox, or your temper. Stand ten minutes daily at the same post with your eyes up and note what moved. Trouble caught early is easier to handle while it is small. Speak warnings plainly before the cost grows.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Heimdallr appears in the Hyndluljóð, Lokasenna, and Völuspá in the Poetic Edda, and in Snorri’s Prose Edda."], 'Name your bridge and your watch-hour. Stand ten minutes daily at the same post with phone away. Give it one dated line daily and one early warning this week.'),
('lore', 'Gods', 'Days of the week', [
 "The Romans invented our seven-day week and named each day for a god or heavenly body: the Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus, and Saturn. In the first centuries CE, Germanic peoples, the ancestors of the Norse and English, began using it.",
 "They kept the week but not the Roman gods. For each Roman god they chose the Germanic god who seemed closest.",
 "<b>Sunday · sunnudagr</b><br>Sól, the sun goddess, who drives the sun across the sky.",
 "<b>Monday · mánadagr</b><br>Máni, the moon god and Sól’s brother, who drives the moon across the sky.",
 "<b>Tuesday · týsdagr</b><br>Týr, god of oaths and law. He was also a god of battle and victory, so he was matched with Rome’s Mars, god of war.",
 "<b>Wednesday · óðinsdagr</b><br>Óðinn, the high one. He wandered in disguise, ruled poetry and speech, and received the dead, so he was matched with Rome’s Mercury, god of speech.",
 "<b>Thursday · þórsdagr</b><br>Þórr, the thunderer. He ruled thunder and carried the hammer Mjölnir, so he was matched with Rome’s Jupiter, who wielded the thunderbolt.",
 "<b>Friday · frjádagr</b><br>Frigg, Óðinn’s wife and goddess of marriage, so she was matched with Rome’s Venus, goddess of love. Some scholars think Freyja.",
 "<b>Saturday · laugardagr</b><br>The Norse named it for bathing, not a god. <i>Laug</i> means “bath,” so <i style=\"hyphens:none\">laugardagr</i> is “washing day.” English kept Rome’s Saturn.",
 "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Poetic Edda and Snorri’s Prose Edda."],
 ''),
('lore', 'Rǫgn: Ruling Powers', 'Jörmungandr: the world serpent', ["Jörmungandr (Jormungandr) is a giant sea serpent. He is one of three monstrous children of Loki and the giantess Angrboða. The other two are the wolf Fenrir and Hel, who rules the dead. In Snorri’s Prose Edda, the gods feared all three, and Óðinn threw the serpent into the deep sea around Miðgarðr (Midgard), the world of people. There he grew until he circled the whole world with his tail in his mouth.",
 "Þórr is his great enemy. In the Hymiskviða (Hymiskvitha), Þórr goes fishing with the giant Hymir and uses an ox head as bait. Jörmungandr bites. Þórr hauls him up to the boat, but Hymir cuts the line, and the serpent sinks back into the sea.",
 "At Ragnarök, Jörmungandr rises from the sea and floods the land. Þórr kills him, then walks nine steps and falls dead from the serpent’s venom.",
 "He is a monster, not a god, and the old sources show no one praying to him. He stands for a danger too big to beat alone. Name one problem you cannot fix this week, and one thing you will do to stay safe around it. Like thin winter ice, some things can be crossed only with care.",
 "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The fishing trip is in the Hymiskviða. His birth, his throwing into the sea, and the final fight are in Snorri’s Prose Edda. This book offers no way to call him."], 'Name the problem you cannot master this season, and the edge you will walk carefully. Lay one store ready and mend one boundary and speak one warning. Give it three dated lines.'),
('lore', 'Ruling Powers', 'Landvættir (Landvaettir): land-spirits', ["Landvættir (landvaettir) are land spirits, the unseen guardians of a place. Icelandic stories and law treat them as real neighbors who must be respected. The Landnámabók, a book of Iceland’s first settlers, says ships had to remove the carved heads from their prows before landing, so the spirits would not be frightened.", "Egils saga shows how seriously people took them. The hero Egill raises a scorn pole, a post topped with a horse’s head, and calls on the land spirits to drive out the king who wronged him.", "The message is plain for any household: you are a guest on the land. Ask leave, keep your landing clean, and do not foul water or wood. Well-kept ground stays kinder than fouled ground, season after season.", "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The ship rule is in the Landnámabók and the scorn pole is in Egils saga. The household lessons were written for this book."], 'Tell me plainly: what ground do you stand on, and which landlord have you never thanked? Clear one path and leave one shore clean. Give it two dated lines.'),
('lore', 'Ruling Powers', 'Landvættir — practice', ['Set aside the first and best part of your walk for the land. Bring a small bag so litter is never left behind. Carry out your litter. Keep to the path. Lower your noise near nesting ground. Share the shore rather than claim it. Keep any care small, and do it right away. Leave the place cleaner than you found it.', 'Count your visits month by month, shore by shore, so the habit stays honest. The pattern is leave-no-trace care, reconstructed from Egils saga and Grágás and fitted to a flat with boots and a bag. What is tended honestly stays welcoming longer than what is only praised.', "Finish the visit inside an hour. Note the date and the place in your log before you go home. Walk the shore and pick up litter. End before supper. Repeat it monthly. Clean shores keep their welcome, the way swept doorsteps keep their visitors.", '<b>Where this comes from:</b> The leave-no-trace practice was rebuilt for this book from Egils saga and Grágás.'], 'This month, what shore will you keep, and what will you carry out? Walk it inside the hour with boots and bag. Give it two dated lines for where and what.'),
('lore', 'Ruling Powers', 'Nornir: The Norns', ["The Nornir (Norns) are the women who shape fate. The Völuspá names three: Urðr (Urd), Verðandi (Verdandi), and Skuld. Their names are often read as what has been, what is becoming, and what must be.", "They live beside the well of Urðr at the foot of Yggdrasill, the world tree. There they carve the lengths of lives and water the tree each day. In Snorri’s Prose Edda, other Norns come to every birth to shape that person’s life, some kind and some cruel.", "Fate and luck are not the same. Fate is the pattern the Norns set, and you cannot choose it. Luck, called hamingja, moves with how you act. Keep your oaths, pay what you owe on time, and give something freely each week.", "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Norns and their well are in the Völuspá in the Poetic Edda. Snorri’s Prose Edda adds the Norns at birth. The idea of luck is in Egils saga."], 'Where stands your luck today: rising, steady, or spent? Name what moved it and one restoring act. Give it two dated lines.'),
('lore', "Ruling Powers", "Valkyries: choosers", ["A valkyrie (Old Norse valkyrja, “chooser of the slain”) is a warrior woman who serves Óðinn. Valkyries ride to battles and choose who will die, then bring the chosen dead to Valhöll, Óðinn’s hall, where they serve ale to the fallen. The Grímnismál names several: Hrist, Mist, and Skeggjöld.", "In the Sigrdrífumál (Sigrdrifumal), the hero Sigurðr wakes a sleeping valkyrie, Sigrdrífa, whom Völsunga saga calls Brynhildr. She teaches him rune counsel: victory runes for the sword, ale runes for the horn, and birth runes for childbirth.", "Her counsel is the part you can use. Before a hard day, ask one elder or steady friend for advice, and say your terms aloud so they can hear. Hard tasks go better with one clear voice beside you.", "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The valkyrie names are in the Grímnismál in the Poetic Edda, and the counsel is in the Sigrdrífumál. This book offers no rites for calling valkyries."], "Name your battle this week, and whose counsel will you seek before it? Ask one elder or steady friend and speak your terms aloud. Give it three dated lines for battle, counsel, and terms."),
('lore', "Ruling Powers", "Blot: simply", ["A blót (blot) was an old offering feast. In Hákonar saga góða, part of Snorri Sturluson’s Heimskringla, people gather and a gift is given. Blood is caught and sprinkled with twigs on the walls and on the people. Toasts are drunk in order: to the gods, to the ancestors, and to victory. Then everyone feasts on the gift itself. The full cup is the toast to the gods, and the minni cup is the toast to memory, passed hand to hand.",
  "The old hall shrinks to a table here, and this book does not pretend to rebuild it. The modern blot has three acts. Set out a gift of water, milk, bread, song, or work, never more than you can spare. Speak aloud the names of who receives it. Eat a share or carry it out, with thanks. Nothing is buried. Nothing is poured where it fouls water. No coins are left in springs.",
  "The whole practice fits inside half an hour. Carry out every trace before you sleep. Repeat it at the next turn of the month. Passing the cup from hand to hand keeps memory alive.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The toasts and blood-sprinkling are in Hákonar saga góða. The household blot was rebuilt for this book."], "What gift will you set out this month, and who receives it by name? Set water or milk or bread or song or work and drink the toast. Give it four dated lines in the tracker."),
('tracker', 'Gods', 'Offering log: what did you give, to whom, and what came back?', ['Water / milk / bread / song / work — date each', 'To whom — name the figure', 'What you asked, in one line', 'What you offered back', 'Leave-no-trace check (carried out?)', 'Week two offering', 'Week three offering', 'Week four offering']),
]

CH3B = [
('lore', 'Seeress and Fate-Work', 'Völva: staff-bearer', ["A völva (volva) is a seeress, a woman who carries a staff and sees what is hidden. The Völuspá is a völva speaking. She is called to the hall of Óðinn and paid to remember. She tells what was: the giants, the dwarfs, and the world tree. Then she tells what comes: winter, breaking, and the last battle. Picture her staff leaning by your chair.",
  "The poem also tells of Heiðr (Heidr), a woman who worked fate craft among households. She was burned three times and born again, never fully welcome and never dismissed.",
  "Read the völva as a witness, not a fortune-teller. The poem gives her words, not her method. No trance steps, drum patterns, or seating plans survive in the Poetic Edda, and this book will not invent them. Guided trance, costume, and ticketed prophecy are modern.",
  "Meet her text honestly at the table with the book open. Read the Völuspá slowly across three sittings, ten stanzas at a time. Write down what it warns about for the week in plain words. Ask one elder for a long memory to set beside it, and date each note so you can see how your questions change. Listening to real people beats buying a fortune.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The seeress speaking is in the Völuspá in the Poetic Edda. Trance scripts and modern divination sold as völva-work are modern."], 'Whose long memory will you ask for this month? Read ten stanzas at a sitting across three sittings. Ask one elder, and write plain dated lines beside the Völuspá.'),
('lore', 'Seeress and Fate-Work', 'Seiðr: fate-work with limits', ["Seiðr (seidr) is a kind of magic tied to fate and spirits, and in the sources it belongs mostly to women. Two mentions in the Poetic Edda carry most of the weight, and both are unkind. In the Völuspá, the seeress says Heiðr practiced seiðr wherever she could, a fate worker moving from house to house. In the Lokasenna, Loki mocks Óðinn: he says Óðinn beat the drum like a seeress on Samsey and went about as a witch among men.",
  "So seiðr is shown as powerful, risky, and mocked in the same breath. That is all the Poetic Edda says. No ritual, step order, dress code, or drum pattern survives. Any staged seiðr with a circle, staff dance, and guided oracle is modern, and so is any lot draw or card read sold as Viking seiðr.",
  "The honest part happens at home. Pick one pattern in your house: late bills, a short temper, or kind help that repeats. Tilt it one degree this week by keeping an oath and giving a gift. A small tilt can turn a long road, the way a rudder turns a ship.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Heiðr is in the Völuspá and the taunt about Samsey is in the Lokasenna, both in the Poetic Edda. Ritual scripts and divination uses are modern."], 'Name one pattern in your house you will tilt one degree this week, and how? Give it oath kept and gift given. Give it three dated lines.'),
('lore', "Seeress and Fate-Work", "Útiseta (Utiseta): sitting out", ["Útiseta (utiseta) means “sitting out.” In Icelandic sagas, a person sits alone at night on a mound or at a crossroads, wrapped in a cloak, to win counsel the household could not give. Verses are sought and dreams are invited. The pattern repeats: alone, overnight, at an edge.",
  "The sagas give the posture, not the steps. No seating plan, staff drill, or summoned voice survives in the sources, and scripted night sittings sold for tickets are modern. Keep it small and safe: a step or garden, with a door unlatched behind you.",
  "Sit once a month for twenty minutes, at dusk or dawn. Carry one question out with you. Do not try to summon anything. Keep your attention open and write down what you noticed.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Mound-sitting is in Icelandic sagas. No ritual survives, and this book offers none."], "What single question will you carry out to the edge, and on which night? Sit twenty minutes at dusk or dawn with phone away. Give it three dated lines for light, sounds, and how you felt, paired with the three day sit log in Liminal Places."),
]



